<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaan Anvelt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaan Anvelt was an Estonian Bolshevik leader who chaired the Commune of the Working People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in November 1918 with Soviet Russian backing during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>. After the Commune collapsed in 1919 he directed the communist underground, including the failed Tallinn uprising of 1924, from Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He was arrested during Stalin's purges and died in custody in 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaan-anvelt.jpg" alt="Jaan Anvelt" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaan_Anvelt_1925.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jaan Anvelt was born on 18 April 1884 into a peasant family in Oorgu, in Võisiku Parish (now Viljandi Parish), in Kreis Fellin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. He trained as a schoolteacher, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> (now Tartu) and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>, where he joined the Bolshevik faction of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party. He worked as a schoolteacher from 1905 to 1907 while taking part in revolutionary activity, and from 1907 to 1912 he studied jurisprudence part-time at St. Petersburg University. As a writer he used several pseudonyms, among them Eessaare Aadu, Jaan Holm and Kaarel Maatamees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 5 November 1917 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorian-calendar">Gregorian calendar</a> (23 October by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a> then still used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>), Anvelt led his revolutionaries in taking political power in Tallinn. He became one of the leaders of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and the first premier of the Soviet Executive Committee of Estonia. After the defeat of the revolutionaries in the Baltic he went underground and remained active, emerging as one of the leaders of the attempted coup of 1924 in Estonia.</p><p>In the USSR, Anvelt served from 1926 to 1929 as political commissar of the Zhukovsky Air Force Engineering Academy. From 1929 to 1935 he held senior positions in the main administration of the civilian air fleet, and from 1935 to 1937 he was a member and executive secretary of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anvelt was arrested in 1937, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>'s Great Purge. Under interrogation in the custody of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> he was beaten by the interrogator Aleksandr Langfang, and he died of his injuries on 11 December 1937; afterwards he was denounced as an enemy of the people. He was rehabilitated in 1956, during the thaw under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>. Langfang was arrested in 1957 and in 1958 sentenced to 15 years in prison for torture and falsifying evidence; he served the whole sentence and was released in 1973.</p><p>Anvelt was married to the Social Democratic politician Alma Ostra from 1909 to 1910, and in 1912 he married a Russian woman, J. Vassiljeva, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>. With his third wife, Alise Stein-Anvelt, he had a daughter, Kima, and a son, Jaan. His grandson Andres Anvelt became Estonian Interior Minister for the Social Democratic Party in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaan Anvelt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The next day Estonian Bolsheviks proclaimed the Commune of the Working People of Estonia there, with <b>Jaan Anvelt</b> as chairman, and Soviet Russia recognised it in December.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-the-working-people-of-estonia">Commune of the Working People of Estonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Viljandi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.33,25.58&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Viljandi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jaan Anvelt"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4802 5136h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4802 5136h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.33,25.58&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
